al conditions: starting with a close-up, and then immediately expanding to a large explosion in the ocean with ships in the foreground. My mind’s already running through the phases - the rapid expansion, the formation of the water column and the base surge, the mushroom cloud's evolution from all angles, including the perspective of the aircraft. I'm focusing on the interplay of water, light, and smoke, mapping each second to a crucial element of the process.\n\nNow, the intervals. This is straightforward: I'm breaking down the initial minute into 60 segments, each starting at a full second and ending just before the next. It’s critical that each caption corresponds to this precise time range: e.g., 00:00:00.000 to 00:00:01.000, and so on.\n\nThe captions have to be punchy and direct, avoiding any redundancy. I have refined them to maintain relevance in less text. For instance, the descriptions should indicate the action happening, without unnecessary fluff. The whole thing needs to be a concise and accurate timeline. My approach involves extracting the most salient visual information from each interval and translating that into the most descriptive words possible. Every piece of the process, from the initial burst to the final dispersion of smoke, needs to be well-accounted for.\n\nFinally, I’ve got to wrap it all up as a neat JSON array. Each element needs to be a well-structured object, containing “start”, “end”, and “caption” fields. That should be a straightforward task after I have all the captions drafted.\n\n\n"}